-- Caves — a hand-drawn level, bigger than any screen, and a camera.
--
-- What this exercises that the other examples do not:
--   * a `level`, so the playfield is the level's 60x30 rather than the screen's
--   * scrolling: the camera follows the hero and clamps at the level's edges
--   * tiles as collision targets, by the names the `.dmtl` legend gave them
--   * `camera.x` / `camera.y`, which is how a HUD stays on screen when the
--     world underneath it moves
--
-- Nothing here is per-console. The level is the same 60x30 everywhere; what
-- changes is how much of it a machine can show at once, and therefore how much
-- it scrolls — a Game Boy sees 20x18 of it, a Mega Drive 40x28.

start title

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- title -----
--
-- The artwork is demade by the image engine at the screen's own size, into a
-- tilemap the background layer draws for nothing — the same fitter a photograph
-- goes through. What it costs is tiles, and the compiler says so when a picture
-- wants more than the game's own art has left.

scene title

backdrop caves.title.svg

create text prompt in title (x centerx - 7, y screenheight - 2, text "press a to play")

when a pressed in title then scene as play


scene play

level cavern from cavern.dmtl

-- The cavern's theme is the one piece in the library with no drums, which is
-- what leaves the arranger a channel to spend on harmony instead.
music hollow.mid in play
sound jump.wav on a pressed in play if footing.value > 0
sound coin.wav on player hits coin
sound hurt.wav on player hits spikes

camera follows player

-- Eleven cells a second, absolute — the same reasoning as the runner's bird, and
-- the opposite of the platformer's hero. A platformer laid out in `vw` is a
-- screenful whatever the machine, so its speeds are relative; this cavern is
-- sixty by thirty cells everywhere, so crossing it should take the same time
-- everywhere, and the jump should clear the same two-cell step.
create object hero (width 1 cell, height 2 cells, speed 11, sprite hero.svg)
create object coin (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, sprite coin.svg)

create hero player in play (x 2, y levelheight - 5)

-- A coin above each ledge of the climb, in level coordinates — absolute, because
-- the cavern is the same sixty by thirty cells on every console and a coin sits
-- on the ledge it sits on.
--
-- Objects, not tiles, and that is the whole point of them: a tile has no
-- `visible`, so a collected coin stayed drawn and scored again the next time the
-- hero entered its cell from outside. Twelve of them in a level four screens
-- wide is only affordable because the backend culls what the view does not
-- cover — see the note on `hits coin` below.
create coin c1 in play (x 5, y 23)
create coin c2 in play (x 13, y 20)
create coin c3 in play (x 23, y 22)
create coin c4 in play (x 41, y 21)
create coin c5 in play (x 32, y 18)
create coin c6 in play (x 19, y 14)
create coin c7 in play (x 49, y 16)
create coin c8 in play (x 34, y 11)
create coin c9 in play (x 10, y 9)
create coin c10 in play (x 46, y 8)
create coin c11 in play (x 25, y 5)
create coin c12 in play (x 41, y 2)


-- Whether the hero has ground under its feet this tick — cleared with the level
-- rules, set by the tile phase, read by the jump after both. Same idiom as the
-- platformer, and for the same reason: a jump that cannot ask is a jump you can
-- press forever.
create number footing in play (value 0, visible 0)

create text scoretag in play (x 1, y 1, text "coins:")
create number score in play (value 0, x 8, y 1)

-- Gravity, as in the platformer: a constant nudge with a terminal speed, so a
-- long fall cannot tunnel through a one-cell ledge between ticks.
when always in play then player.ydirection as min(player.ydirection + 0.04, 0.9)
when always in play then footing.value as 0

control player left (xdirection -1) on hold
control player right (xdirection 1) on hold

-- `screenleft` and friends are the *level's* edges once a scene has a level, so
-- this stops the hero at the end of the cavern, not a screen-width in.
when player hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0

-- Landing, and bonking your head. `touches`, not `hits`, for the same reason as
-- in the platformer: resting on a ledge is a state, and gravity has to be
-- cancelled every tick of it or it accumulates behind the separation. The guard
-- separates arriving from below (a bonk, which only stops the climb) from
-- arriving from above (a landing, which also gives you something to jump from).
when player touches ledge if player.ydirection >= 0 then (ydirection, footing.value) as (0, 1) else ydirection as 0

-- A wall stops a climb *once*, on contact. With `touches` here instead, standing
-- against the side of the shaft would cancel gravity every tick and the hero
-- would hang on the wall — and, because a wall is not something you stand on,
-- it grants no footing to jump from either.
when player hits wall then ydirection as 0

when a pressed if footing.value > 0 then player.ydirection as -1.5

-- Tile names, straight from the legend. Spikes are not `solid`, so the hero
-- falls into them rather than standing on them.
when player touches spikes then scene as gameover
when player hits exit then scene as won

-- A collected coin is gone for good, which is what `visible 0` is for: not
-- drawn, not collided with, not moved.
--
-- Twelve collectibles in a scrolling level is more than the tick used to hold.
-- What made it fit is that the cavern is four screens wide, so eleven of the
-- twelve are off the view at any moment — and the backend now says so cheaply,
-- in whole cells, before it stages a box or builds a sprite. Work you can prove
-- is invisible is work you do not have to do.
when player hits coin then (coin.visible, score.value) as (0, score.value + 1)

-- The HUD is two ordinary objects at level coordinates, so without this they
-- would be left behind the moment the view moved. Pinning them to the camera is
-- one line, and it is the only thing in the game that knows the view exists.
--
-- The backend then has to make that pin *look* pinned: a scene that scrolls
-- draws its counters with sprites rather than background cells, because the
-- background moves as one piece and a cell of it cannot be held still while the
-- rest of it slides.
when always in play then (scoretag.x, scoretag.y, score.x, score.y) as (camera.x + 1, camera.y + 1, camera.x + 8, camera.y + 1)

-- --------------------------------------------------------------- endings ----

scene gameover

create text lost in gameover (x centerx - 4, y centery, text "you fell")

when a pressed in gameover then scene as play

scene won

create text escaped in won (x centerx - 5, y centery, text "you got out")

when a pressed in won then scene as play
